### Why Model Serialization is Critical
The Security Problem Without It, When we serialize a job containing Entity ORM models without Model Serialization:
```php
class SendEmailJob extends Job
{
    // WITHOUT InteractsWithModelSerialization trait
    protected $user; // User model instance
    
    public function __construct(User $user)
    {
        $this->user = $user;
    }
}

// When dispatched:
$user = User::find(1);
$job = new SendEmailJob($user);
serialize($job); // Serializes ENTIRE user object!
```
#### What gets serialized:

- ALL model attributes (including hidden ones)
- Password hashes
- Remember tokens
- API keys
- All loaded relationships
- Timestamps
- Everything in `$attributes` array

### Security Risks:
- Data Exposure: Hidden/protected fields are serialized in plain text in the database
- Stale Data: User data might change between dispatch and execution
- Large Payloads: Full models with relationships bloat queue storage
- Password Leaks: Even hashed passwords shouldn't be in queue tables

### The Solution: InteractsWithModelSerialization
With InteractsWithModelSerialization trait (now built into Job base class):

```php
class SendEmailJob extends Job
{
    use Dispatchable;
    // InteractsWithModelSerialization already included in Job base class
    
    protected $user; // User model instance
    
    public function __construct(User $user)
    {
        $this->user = $user;
    }
}

// When dispatched:
$user = User::find(1);
$job = new SendEmailJob($user);
serialize($job); // Only stores: ['class' => 'App\Models\User', 'id' => 1]
```

### What gets serialized:
- ✓ Only the model class name
- ✓ Only the primary key (ID)
- ✓ Connection name (if using multiple databases)
- ✓ Relationship metadata (IDs only, not data)

### When job runs:
- Model is freshly fetched from database: User::find(1)
- Always gets current data
- No stale information
- No security leaks

No changes needed. All jobs now automatically use InteractsWithModelSerialization

App.tsx contained 1911 lines with all page components, form modals, and business logic in a single file, making maintenance and navigation difficult.

## Changes

**Extracted page components** (12 files in `components/pages/`)
- DashboardPage, MembersPage, PrayerTimesPage, AnnouncementsPage, DonationsPage, EventsPage
- MosquesPage, AuditLogPage, LoginScreen, LandingPage, AdminProfilePage

**Extracted form components** (6 files in `components/forms/`)
- MemberFormModal, PrayerTimeFormModal, AnnouncementFormModal
- DonationFormModal, EventFormModal, MosqueFormModal

**Extracted shared utilities** (`components/utils/formHelpers.ts`)
- Type-safe form change handlers
- Common event handler types

**Simplified App.tsx** (182 lines, down from 1911)
- Retained only routing logic and state management
- Clean imports via index files

## Example

Before:
```typescript
// App.tsx (1911 lines)
const DashboardPage = ({ mosque }: { mosque: Mosque }) => { /* 200+ lines */ }
const MembersPage = ({ mosque }: { mosque: Mosque }) => { /* 150+ lines */ }
// ... 9 more pages, 7 modals, utilities all in one file
```

After:
```typescript
// App.tsx (182 lines)
import { DashboardPage, MembersPage, /* ... */ } from './components/pages';
import { MemberFormModal, /* ... */ } from './components/forms';

function App() {
  const renderPage = () => {
    switch (currentPage) {
      case 'dashboard': return <DashboardPage mosque={selectedMosque} />;
      // ...
    }
  }
}
```

Component files average 100 lines each (largest: 215 lines).

